<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="p1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\headertitle</span>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p">PRISM
<span id="p1.2.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\headerauthors</span>Hu et al.</p>
</div>
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">PRISM: Probabilistic and Robust Inverse Solver with Measurement-Conditioned Diffusion Prior for Blind Inverse Problems</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yuanyun Hu<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1,2</span></sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Evan Bell<sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Guijin Wang<sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">and Yu Sun<sup id="id4" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1,</span></sup><sup id="id5" class="ltx_sup">✉</sup>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span><sup id="id6" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>Johns Hopkins University  <sup id="id7" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Tsinghua University
<br class="ltx_break"><sup id="id8" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">✉</span></sup><span id="id9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Corresponding author: ysun214@jh.edu</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models are now commonly used to solve inverse problems in computational imaging.
However, most diffusion-based inverse solvers require complete knowledge of the forward operator to be used.
In this work, we introduce a novel <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">probabilistic and robust inverse solver with measurement-conditioned diffusion prior (PRISM)</span> to effectively address <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">blind</span> inverse problems.
PRISM offers a technical advancement over current methods by incorporating a powerful measurement-conditioned diffusion model into a theoretically principled posterior sampling scheme.
Experiments on blind image deblurring validate the effectiveness of the proposed method, demonstrating the superior performance of PRISM over state-of-the-art baselines in both image and blur kernel recovery.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para"><span id="p2.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\blfootnote</span>
<p id="p2.2" class="ltx_p">Yuanyun Hu is a student in the Tsinghua–JHU-BME Dual Degree Program. The work was done when Yuanyun Hu studied at Johns Hopkins University. Evan Bell is supported by the U.S. Department of Energy Computational Science Graduate Fellowship.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In computational imaging, reconstructing an image when the forward model is partially <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unknown</span> remains a significant challenge.
Common examples of such unknowns in real applications include the sensitivity map in magnetic resonance image (MRI) reconstruction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, accurate view angles in X-ray computed tomography (CT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and the blur kernel in image deblurring <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.
Mathematically, the reconstruction task can be formulated as a <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">blind inverse problem</span></p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}=H_{\bm{\varphi}}\bm{x}+\bm{n},\quad\bm{n}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\sigma_{\bm{y}}^{2}\bm{I})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒏</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒏</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}=H_{\bm{\varphi}}\bm{x}+\bm{n},\quad\bm{n}\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\sigma_{\bm{y}}^{2}\bm{I}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the true underlying signal, <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is the observed measurement, <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\bm{\varphi}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\bm{\varphi}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the forward matrix with unknown parameters <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is Gaussian measurement noise.
The goal here is to recover <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> as accurately as possible from <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, which typically involves jointly estimating <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models have recently emerged as an effective tool for solving inverse problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.
Several methods have been developed for blind inverse problems using diffusion models, such as <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">BlindDPS</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GibbsDDRM</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, and <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Kernel-Diff</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.
Despite their effectiveness, each of these methods comes with certain limitations.
For example, BlindDPS uses an <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unconditional</span> diffusion model as a prior for estimating <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math>, leaving the rich information about <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> contained in <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> unutilized.
Similarly, although GibbsDDRM is more theoretically principled, it relies on a simple Laplace prior for <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math>, which often results in unsatisfactory recovery.
Finally, while Kernel-Diff uses a conditional diffusion model for estimating <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, it resorts to using a traditional non-blind deep network to predict <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math> for a fixed estimate of <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the diffusion model is not used as an image prior, any inaccuracies in estimating <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> may propagate into substantial errors in the estimate of <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we develop a novel diffusion-based blind inverse solver termed <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">probabilistic and robust inverse solver with measurement-conditioned diffusion prior (PRISM)</span> by extending the <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Plug-and-Play Diffusion Models (PnP-DM)</span> framework introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> to the blind setting.
The resulting approach overcomes the major limitations of BlindDPS, GibbsDDRM, and Kernel-Diff while retaining the best features of each of these algorithms.
In particular, the proposed PRISM uses diffusion models as priors for reconstructing both <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> (as in BlindDPS), employs a theoretically principled sampling scheme (as in GibbsDDRM), and leverages a powerful <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">conditional</span> diffusion model to effectively estimate <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> (as in Kernel-Diff).
We note that a similarly derived method, <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Blind-PnPDM</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, was recently proposed; however we demonstrate that PRISM’s use of a <span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">measurement-conditioned</span> kernel prior offers substantial improvements over Blind-PnPDM in terms of both performance and robustness.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16106v1/compare.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/159;" width="452" height="159" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Visual comparison of the image (<math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math>) and kernel (<math id="S1.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math>) reconstructions obtained by PRISM and baseline methods for the blind motion deblurring task (<math id="S1.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>). Each reconstruction shown is a single sample generated by each method (single-sample estimation). Note how PRISM recovers fine textures such as hair and skin wrinkles while also retaining high PSNR and SSIM values.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Blind Bayesian Inverse Problem</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From a Bayesian perspective, solving blind inverse problems can be viewed as inferring the joint posterior distribution</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x},\bm{\varphi}\mid\bm{y})\propto p(\bm{y}\mid\bm{x},\bm{\varphi})\,p(\bm{x})\,p(\bm{\varphi})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝋</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝋</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝋</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x},\bm{\varphi}\mid\bm{y})\propto p(\bm{y}\mid\bm{x},\bm{\varphi})\,p(\bm{x})\,p(\bm{\varphi}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the likelihood <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{y}\mid\bm{x},\bm{\varphi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝋</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{y}\mid\bm{x},\bm{\varphi})</annotation></semantics></math> enforces data fidelity, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math> is the prior of the image, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{\varphi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝋</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{\varphi})</annotation></semantics></math> is the prior of the unknown parameters.
In the negative log domain, we denote <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\bm{x},\bm{\varphi};\bm{y})=-\log p(\bm{y}\mid\bm{x},\bm{\varphi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝋</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\bm{x},\bm{\varphi};\bm{y})=-\log p(\bm{y}\mid\bm{x},\bm{\varphi})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\bm{x}}(\bm{x})=-\log p(\bm{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>𝒙</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\bm{x}}(\bm{x})=-\log p(\bm{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\bm{\varphi}}(\bm{\varphi})=-\log p(\bm{\varphi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝋</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝋</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\bm{\varphi}}(\bm{\varphi})=-\log p(\bm{\varphi})</annotation></semantics></math>, giving</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x},\bm{\varphi}\mid\bm{y})\propto\exp\!\left(-g(\bm{x},\bm{\varphi};\bm{y})-r_{\bm{x}}(\bm{x})-r_{\bm{\varphi}}(\bm{\varphi})\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝋</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>𝒙</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝋</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x},\bm{\varphi}\mid\bm{y})\propto\exp\!\left(-g(\bm{x},\bm{\varphi};\bm{y})-r_{\bm{x}}(\bm{x})-r_{\bm{\varphi}}(\bm{\varphi})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Directly sampling <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x},\bm{\varphi}\mid\bm{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝋</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x},\bm{\varphi}\mid\bm{y})</annotation></semantics></math> is difficult due to the strong coupling between <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> and the nonconvex nature of the problem.
To overcome this, we adapt the PnP-DM framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, which utilizes a <span id="S2.SS1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">split Gibbs sampling (SGS)</span> strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> to decouple the likelihood and priors.
This is accomplished by introducing auxiliary variables <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒎</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> and considering the augmented distribution</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\pi(\bm{x},\bm{z},\bm{\varphi},\bm{m}\mid\bm{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒎</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi(\bm{x},\bm{z},\bm{\varphi},\bm{m}\mid\bm{y})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\exp\bigg(-g(\bm{z},\bm{m};\bm{y})-r_{\bm{x}}(\bm{x})-r_{\bm{\varphi}}(\bm{\varphi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒎</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>𝒙</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝋</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\exp\bigg(-g(\bm{z},\bm{m};\bm{y})-r_{\bm{x}}(\bm{x})-r_{\bm{\varphi}}(\bm{\varphi})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad-\frac{\|\bm{x}-\bm{z}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\rho_{\bm{x}}^{2}}-\frac{\|\bm{\varphi}-\bm{m}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{2}}\bigg)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒎</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝝋</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad-\frac{\|\bm{x}-\bm{z}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\rho_{\bm{x}}^{2}}-\frac{\|\bm{\varphi}-\bm{m}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{2}}\bigg),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\bm{x}},\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝒙</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\bm{x}},\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> control the coupling between <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{x},\bm{z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{x},\bm{z})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{\varphi},\bm{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{\varphi},\bm{m})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The advantage of sampling from this distribution, rather than directly from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x},\bm{\varphi}\mid\bm{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝋</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x},\bm{\varphi}\mid\bm{y})</annotation></semantics></math>, is that the resulting updates for <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> only involve the prior, while the corresponding updates for <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{m}</annotation></semantics></math> only involve the likelihood.
This strategy is analogous to the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">variable splitting</span> technique used in Half-Quadratic Splitting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and ADMM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, which has been shown to be effective for non-convex problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Proposed Method: PRISM</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed PRISM aims to sample from (<a href="#S2.Ex1" title="In 2.1 Blind Bayesian Inverse Problem ‣ 2 Method ‣ PRISM: Probabilistic and Robust Inverse Solver with Measurement-Conditioned Diffusion Prior for Blind Inverse Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) by alternating between four conditional updates, which are outlined in the remainder of this section.
In each update step, only one variable is updated while the others are kept fixed.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> PRISM</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            Initialization <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}^{0},\bm{m}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒎</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}^{0},\bm{m}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>; total iterations <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>; coupling schedules <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\rho_{\bm{x}}^{k}\}_{k=1}^{K},\{\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝝋</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\rho_{\bm{x}}^{k}\}_{k=1}^{K},\{\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>; likelihood potential <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\,\cdot\,;\bm{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\,\cdot\,;\bm{y})</annotation></semantics></math>; pretrained image diffusion model <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{D}^{\bm{x}}(\,\cdot\,)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝒙</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{D}^{\bm{x}}(\,\cdot\,)</annotation></semantics></math> (unconditional) and kernel diffusion model <math id="alg1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{D}^{\bm{\varphi}}(\,\cdot\,;\bm{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{D}^{\bm{\varphi}}(\,\cdot\,;\bm{y})</annotation></semantics></math> (conditional on <math id="alg1.l1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}</annotation></semantics></math>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{x}^{K},\bm{\varphi}^{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝝋</mi><mi>K</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{x}^{K},\bm{\varphi}^{K})</annotation></semantics></math> as an approximate sample from <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(\bm{x},\bm{\varphi}\mid\bm{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝋</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(\bm{x},\bm{\varphi}\mid\bm{y})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,2,\dots,K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,2,\dots,K</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}^{k}\leftarrow\texttt{KernelCondPrior}\!\left(\bm{m}^{k-1},\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{k},\mathsf{D}^{\bm{\varphi}}(\,\cdot\,;\bm{y})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝝋</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:8.039em;" width="8.039em"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">KernelCondPrior</mtext></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝒎</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝝋</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}^{k}\leftarrow\texttt{KernelCondPrior}\!\left(\bm{m}^{k-1},\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{k},\mathsf{D}^{\bm{\varphi}}(\,\cdot\,;\bm{y})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}^{k}\leftarrow\texttt{ImageLikelihood}\!\left(\bm{x}^{k-1},\bm{\varphi}^{k},\rho_{\bm{x}}^{k},g(\,\cdot\,;\bm{y})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:7.505em;" width="7.505em"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ImageLikelihood</mtext></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝝋</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}^{k}\leftarrow\texttt{ImageLikelihood}\!\left(\bm{x}^{k-1},\bm{\varphi}^{k},\rho_{\bm{x}}^{k},g(\,\cdot\,;\bm{y})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}^{k}\leftarrow\texttt{ImagePrior}\!\left(\bm{z}^{k},\rho_{\bm{x}}^{k},\mathsf{D}^{\bm{x}}(\,\cdot\,)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:5.301em;" width="5.301em"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">ImagePrior</mtext></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝒙</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}^{k}\leftarrow\texttt{ImagePrior}\!\left(\bm{z}^{k},\rho_{\bm{x}}^{k},\mathsf{D}^{\bm{x}}(\,\cdot\,)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{m}^{k}\leftarrow\texttt{KernelLikelihood}\!\left(\bm{x}^{k},\bm{\varphi}^{k},\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{k},g(\,\cdot\,;\bm{y})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒎</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:7.851em;" width="7.851em"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">KernelLikelihood</mtext></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝝋</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝝋</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{m}^{k}\leftarrow\texttt{KernelLikelihood}\!\left(\bm{x}^{k},\bm{\varphi}^{k},\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{k},g(\,\cdot\,;\bm{y})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kernel Conditional Prior Step.</span> Given <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, the kernel <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> is updated by sampling the distribution</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{\varphi}\mid\bm{m})\propto\exp\!\left(-r_{\bm{\varphi}}(\bm{\varphi})-\frac{\|\bm{\varphi}-\bm{m}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{2}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝋</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝋</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒎</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝝋</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{\varphi}\mid\bm{m})\propto\exp\!\left(-r_{\bm{\varphi}}(\bm{\varphi})-\frac{\|\bm{\varphi}-\bm{m}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{2}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The key insight in the PnP-DM framework is that right hand side of (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.2 Proposed Method: PRISM ‣ 2 Method ‣ PRISM: Probabilistic and Robust Inverse Solver with Measurement-Conditioned Diffusion Prior for Blind Inverse Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) is the likelihood of a Gaussian denoising problem with noise variance <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝝋</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, prior <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{\varphi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝋</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{\varphi})</annotation></semantics></math>, and noisy observation <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Explicitly, this can be seen by rewriting the right hand side as</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp\!\left(-r_{\bm{\varphi}}(\bm{\varphi})-\frac{\|\bm{\varphi}-\bm{m}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{2}}\right)\propto p(\bm{\varphi})\mathcal{N}(\bm{\varphi};\bm{m},\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{2}\bm{I})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝋</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒎</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝝋</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝋</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒎</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝝋</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp\!\left(-r_{\bm{\varphi}}(\bm{\varphi})-\frac{\|\bm{\varphi}-\bm{m}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{2}}\right)\propto p(\bm{\varphi})\mathcal{N}(\bm{\varphi};\bm{m},\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{2}\bm{I}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">It is then straightforward to sample from this distribution using a diffusion model. In particular, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> showed that one can simply initialize the reverse process with <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> (up to a scaling factor) and then run the reverse process from an appropriately chosen time point corresponding to a noise level of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> for complete details of how this can be performed with arbitrary diffusion models. In PRISM, we implement this step with a <span id="S2.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">measurement-conditioned</span> diffusion model denoted by <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{D}^{\bm{\varphi}}(\,\cdot\,;\text{$\bm{y}$})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{D}^{\bm{\varphi}}(\,\cdot\,;\text{$\bm{y}$})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Empirically, we found that measurement conditioning is not a minor design choice, but is critical to the success of the proposed approach.
This is demonstrated in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1 Experimental setup ‣ 3 Numerical Validation ‣ PRISM: Probabilistic and Robust Inverse Solver with Measurement-Conditioned Diffusion Prior for Blind Inverse Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, where we show that Blind-PnPDM, which is conceptually similar to PRISM but uses an unconditional kernel prior, yields poor convergence and fails to find a reasonable solution when <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math> is initialized randomly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image Likelihood Step.</span> Given <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math>, the latent <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn from</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{z}\mid\bm{y},\bm{\varphi},\bm{x})\propto\exp\!\left(-\frac{\|H_{\bm{\varphi}}\bm{z}-\bm{y}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\sigma_{\bm{y}}^{2}}-\frac{\|\bm{x}-\bm{z}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\rho_{\bm{x}}^{2}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{z}\mid\bm{y},\bm{\varphi},\bm{x})\propto\exp\!\left(-\frac{\|H_{\bm{\varphi}}\bm{z}-\bm{y}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\sigma_{\bm{y}}^{2}}-\frac{\|\bm{x}-\bm{z}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\rho_{\bm{x}}^{2}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\bm{\varphi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\bm{\varphi}}</annotation></semantics></math> is linear, this distribution is Gaussian with covariance and mean specified by</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\bm{z}}^{-1}=\frac{1}{\sigma_{\bm{y}}^{2}}H_{\bm{\varphi}}^{\top}H_{\bm{\varphi}}+\frac{1}{\rho_{\bm{x}}^{2}}I,\quad\mu_{z}=\bm{\Sigma}_{\bm{z}}\!\left(\frac{1}{\sigma_{\bm{y}}^{2}}H_{\bm{\varphi}}^{\top}\bm{y}+\frac{1}{\rho_{\bm{x}}^{2}}\bm{x}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>𝒛</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\bm{z}}^{-1}=\frac{1}{\sigma_{\bm{y}}^{2}}H_{\bm{\varphi}}^{\top}H_{\bm{\varphi}}+\frac{1}{\rho_{\bm{x}}^{2}}I,\quad\mu_{z}=\bm{\Sigma}_{\bm{z}}\!\left(\frac{1}{\sigma_{\bm{y}}^{2}}H_{\bm{\varphi}}^{\top}\bm{y}+\frac{1}{\rho_{\bm{x}}^{2}}\bm{x}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Importantly, all of the operations in (<a href="#S2.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ 2.2 Proposed Method: PRISM ‣ 2 Method ‣ PRISM: Probabilistic and Robust Inverse Solver with Measurement-Conditioned Diffusion Prior for Blind Inverse Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) can be implemented efficiently using the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT).
For non-linear <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\bm{\varphi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\bm{\varphi}}</annotation></semantics></math> or non-Gaussian noise, gradient-based MCMC methods such as Langevin dynamics can be used to effectively draw samples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image Prior Step.</span> Given <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, the image <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is updated by sampling from</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{x}\mid\bm{z})\propto\exp\left(-r_{\bm{x}}(\bm{x})-\frac{\|\bm{x}-\bm{z}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\rho_{\bm{x}}^{2}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>𝒙</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{x}\mid\bm{z})\propto\exp\left(-r_{\bm{x}}(\bm{x})-\frac{\|\bm{x}-\bm{z}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\rho_{\bm{x}}^{2}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">The implementation of this step is essentially identical to the <span id="S2.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Kernel Prior Step</span>, and is achieved by running the reverse process of a pretrained diffusion model, which we denote by <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{D}^{\bm{x}}(\,\cdot\,)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖣</mi><mi>𝒙</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{D}^{\bm{x}}(\,\cdot\,)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kernel Likelihood Step.</span> Given <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\varphi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\varphi}</annotation></semantics></math>, the kernel <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn from</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{m}\mid\bm{y},\bm{\varphi},\bm{x})\propto\exp\!\left(-\frac{\|H_{\bm{m}}\bm{x}-\bm{y}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\sigma_{\bm{y}}^{2}}-\frac{\|\bm{m}-\bm{\varphi}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{2}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒎</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝒎</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒎</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝝋</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝝋</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{m}\mid\bm{y},\bm{\varphi},\bm{x})\propto\exp\!\left(-\frac{\|H_{\bm{m}}\bm{x}-\bm{y}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\sigma_{\bm{y}}^{2}}-\frac{\|\bm{m}-\bm{\varphi}\|_{2}^{2}}{2\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{2}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">As in the image likelihood step, this distribution is Gaussian, and its mean and covariance are available in closed form.
This can be seen by using the fact that convolution is commutative to treat <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> as the kernel and <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{m}</annotation></semantics></math> as the signal.
In matrix form, we can write <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{\bm{m}}\bm{x}=C_{\bm{x}}\bm{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>𝒎</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝒙</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒎</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{\bm{m}}\bm{x}=C_{\bm{x}}\bm{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝒙</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an appropriate Toeplitz matrix constructed from <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then obtain the covariance and mean exactly as in the image likelihood step</p>
<table id="S2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\Sigma}_{\bm{m}}^{-1}=\frac{1}{\sigma_{\bm{y}}^{2}}C_{\bm{x}}^{\top}C_{\bm{x}}+\frac{1}{\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{2}}I,\quad\bm{\mu}_{\bm{m}}=\bm{\Sigma}_{\bm{m}}\left(\frac{1}{\sigma_{\bm{y}}^{2}}C_{\bm{x}}^{\top}\bm{y}+\frac{1}{\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{2}}\bm{\varphi}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>𝒎</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>𝒙</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝝋</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>𝒎</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>𝒎</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝝋</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝝋</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\Sigma}_{\bm{m}}^{-1}=\frac{1}{\sigma_{\bm{y}}^{2}}C_{\bm{x}}^{\top}C_{\bm{x}}+\frac{1}{\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{2}}I,\quad\bm{\mu}_{\bm{m}}=\bm{\Sigma}_{\bm{m}}\left(\frac{1}{\sigma_{\bm{y}}^{2}}C_{\bm{x}}^{\top}\bm{y}+\frac{1}{\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{2}}\bm{\varphi}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.3" class="ltx_p">More details on how to efficiently compute these quantities and sample from <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mu}_{\bm{m}},\bm{\Sigma}_{\bm{m}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>𝒎</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>𝒎</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mu}_{\bm{m}},\bm{\Sigma}_{\bm{m}})</annotation></semantics></math> can be found in Appendix C of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The complete PRISM procedure is summarized in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.2 Proposed Method: PRISM ‣ 2 Method ‣ PRISM: Probabilistic and Robust Inverse Solver with Measurement-Conditioned Diffusion Prior for Blind Inverse Problems" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
We initialize <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{m}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒎</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{m}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, then iterate the four updates with coupling parameters <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\bm{x}}^{k},\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>𝝋</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\bm{x}}^{k},\rho_{\bm{\varphi}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> annealed from large to small values.
This annealing accelerates chain mixing and helps escape poor local minima in highly ill-posed blind inverse problems.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Numerical Validation</h2>

<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Numerical results obtained by PRISM and baselines for single-sample estimation. All values are averaged over the test dataset. RMSE values are in <math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> units. <span id="S3.T1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bold</span> marks best results; <span id="S3.T1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlined</span> numbers indicate second best.</figcaption>
